{
  "result": "The library at the end of my street opens before sunrise. Its windows glow softly while the rest of the neighborhood sleeps. Inside, a clock ticks above the desk, and someone has placed a bowl of imperfect apples beside the return slot. Nobody seems to know who brings them there.\n\nI like to imagine the apples arrive from a garden that no longer appears on any map. Perhaps an old woman carries them in her coat pockets, or a baker leaves them on the way to work. The truth is probably simpler, which makes the little mystery no less lovely.\n\nOn rainy mornings, I choose a book without checking its title. This small rule has led me to atlases, cookbooks, plays, and a manual on repairing clocks. Most were not what I needed, or so I thought, until a sentence or illustration changed the direction of my entire day unexpectedly.\n\nThe librarians rarely interrupt these explorations. They understand that wandering is sometimes a form of attention. A person staring at a shelf may be searching for a fact or for permission to become interested in something new. Either way, the silence around them is not empty but quietly, gently alive.\n\nWhen I leave, I sometimes take an apple. Outside, the street has filled with buses, dogs, deliveries, and people hurrying toward their obligations. I carry the fruit home in my pocket, feeling its reassuring weight. By evening, I have usually forgotten the book but remembered that anonymous act of generosity.",
